rules.requirement:
Rental housing providers in Berkeley may not ask about or use criminal history or criminal background checks in rental advertising, applications, tenant selection or decision-making. As to current or prospective tenants, it is unlawful to:
- inquire about criminal history;
- indicate that people with criminal backgrounds will not be considered;
- refuse to rent, or terminate a tenancy, based on criminal history;
- require disclosure of, or authorization to release, criminal history;
- demand a higher security deposit or rent based on criminal history;
- refuse to add an immediate family member because of that person's criminal history;
- disqualify tenants from rental assistance programs such as Section 8 based on criminal history (subject to exceptions);
- treat applicants or tenants differently based on criminal history.
A landlord who takes any action based on criminal history must give the applicant or tenant written notice and an opportunity to respond. Housing providers must post the Notice to Rental Applicants and Tenants prominently on application materials, on websites and at locations they control that applicants frequently visit. The Rent Board administers complaints.
